### Testing Shared Components Across Module Federation Micro-Frontends

Module Federation lets independently built and deployed applications share code at runtime rather than at build time — components, hooks, utilities. A "host" application loads a "remote" module dynamically. It often resolves the actual version of a shared dependency only when the app runs in the browser. That dependency can be React, or a design-system library.

This introduces a class of bugs that don't exist in a monolith. Each micro-frontend (MFE) can have a perfectly green isolated test suite. The application can still break in production, because of how the *pieces* combine at runtime.

The two risks specific to this architecture:

1. **Shared dependency version mismatches.** Suppose MFE A was built against React 18.2, and MFE B — which it loads at runtime — was built against React 18.0. Module Federation's singleton sharing can silently resolve to one version at runtime. Subtle behavioral differences follow, or worse, two copies of React load because the singleton config is wrong. No single MFE's isolated test suite catches this, because each one tests fine against its *own* dependency version.
2. **Contract drift on the shared interface.** A shared component or hook exposed by one team can change its props or API. That quietly breaks a consuming MFE owned by a different team. Each MFE deploys independently, so no single build step asks whether this change breaks a consumer. A monorepo would fail the build on a type error; here nothing does.

```typescript
// Consuming a remote module — this import is resolved at runtime, not build time
const RemoteButton = lazy(() => import('remoteApp/Button'));

// A component-level test in the consuming MFE's own suite passes...
test('renders the checkout button', () => {
  render(<Suspense fallback={null}><RemoteButton label="Pay" /></Suspense>);
  // ...but this test used whatever version of RemoteButton was available
  // at test time (often mocked or pinned), not necessarily what's actually
  // deployed for the remote in production right now.
});
```

The practical mitigation strategy has three layers.

- **Contract tests on the host/remote interface.** Alternatively, publish generated TypeScript types from the shared library. Either way, a breaking prop change fails the *provider's* CI (continuous integration) pipeline, instead of being discovered by the consumer in production.
- **Integration smoke tests that load the real deployed remote.** Point them at a staging or canary address, not at a local mock. Only these catch real version-resolution problems at runtime.
- **Strict `shared` version ranges in the Module Federation config**, with a CI alert when versions drift apart.

**Common pitfall:** Testing each micro-frontend in isolation, with every remote import mocked. A green suite in every MFE is then treated as proof the whole application works. This is exactly the gap that Module Federation's runtime composition creates. Every piece can be green on its own while the real runtime combination breaks, because no test suite ever exercised the real integration point.

