### Mutation Testing: Measuring Test Suite Effectiveness

Code coverage answers one question: did my tests *execute* this line? It says nothing about whether the tests would actually *notice* if that line's logic were wrong.

Mutation testing attacks that blind spot directly. A tool — Stryker for JS and TS — automatically introduces small deliberate bugs into your source code. These are called "mutants": a `>` flipped to `>=`, an `&&` changed to `||`, a replaced return value. The tool then re-runs your test suite against each mutated version.

If at least one test fails, the mutant is "killed": your suite caught the bug. If every test still passes, the mutant "survived". That means your tests exercised the code path without checking anything meaningful about it.

```typescript
// Original code
function isEligibleForDiscount(age: number, memberYears: number) {
  return age >= 65 || memberYears > 10;
}

// A mutant Stryker might generate: changed >= to >
function isEligibleForDiscount(age: number, memberYears: number) {
  return age > 65 || memberYears > 10;  // mutant: boundary shifted
}

// If this test suite doesn't include a boundary case at age === 65,
// the mutant survives — coverage would still show 100% for this line,
// but the test suite can't actually distinguish >= from >
it('is eligible past the age threshold', () => {
  expect(isEligibleForDiscount(70, 0)).toBe(true); // doesn't test the boundary
});

// A test that would kill the mutant above:
it('is eligible exactly at the age threshold', () => {
  expect(isEligibleForDiscount(65, 0)).toBe(true); // catches >= vs > boundary bugs
});
```

The output is a **mutation score**: the percentage of mutants killed. It is a meaningfully different number from a coverage percentage, and usually much harder to inflate. A codebase can sit at 95% line coverage and 40% mutation score at the same time. The mutation score is the more honest signal about whether refactoring that code safely is actually protected by tests.

The trade-off is cost. Mutation testing runs your entire test suite once per mutant, so it is computationally expensive: minutes to hours, depending on codebase size. You do not run it on every commit. Teams typically run it nightly or on a schedule, or scope it to the files touched in a pull request (PR). It is not a blocking check on every push.

**Common pitfall:** Chasing a high mutation score as a target, the way teams sometimes chase a coverage percentage. A mutation score is a diagnostic for finding weak spots in an existing suite. It is not a KPI (key performance indicator) to game. Writing tests specifically to kill trivial mutants, rather than to verify meaningful behavior, produces the same false confidence as chasing raw coverage numbers.

